    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-64985",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14110945/assets/0beb8842ac4b8781a90973a5f56d4c8b5fc27f42af549fe23a9f9042b3067c9b.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14110945/assets/7895e1fbe54cd3dea65ba1c75d79c6f325f14cd6bc55f9c7ad99299f00b516df.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{2}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$",
